# Semiconductors and Electronics: From Doped Silicon to Logic Gates

> Every computer, phone, and charger is built from one idea: a
> material whose conductivity can be dialled between conductor and
> insulator. This lesson follows that idea from doped silicon through
> diodes and transistors to the logic gates that compute.

## 1. Conductors, Insulators, Semiconductors

The difference is the **band gap** — the energy an electron needs to
jump from the bound valence band to the free conduction band.

| Material | Band gap | Conductivity | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Conductor | Overlapping bands | Very high | Copper, silver, aluminium |
| Semiconductor | Small (~1 eV) | In between, tunable | Silicon, germanium |
| Insulator | Large (> 5 eV) | Negligible | Glass, rubber, diamond |

[definition]
A **semiconductor** conducts poorly at low temperature but much better
when warm, doped, or lit — its conductivity rises with temperature,
the exact opposite of a metal.
[/definition]

Silicon (group 14, four valence electrons) is the workhorse because
it forms a perfect crystal lattice with each atom sharing electrons
with four neighbours.

## 2. Doping: n-type and p-type

Pure (intrinsic) silicon carries almost no free charge. Adding a
trace impurity — **doping** — changes everything:

- **n-type:** inject a group-15 atom (phosphorus, arsenic) with five
  valence electrons. Four bond, the fifth roams free — the charge
  carriers are **electrons** (negative).
- **p-type:** inject a group-13 atom (boron, gallium) with three
  valence electrons. One bond is left incomplete — a **hole** that
  behaves as a mobile positive charge carrier.

[important]
Both types are electrically neutral overall — doping adds *carriers*,
not net charge. In n-type the majority carriers are electrons; in
p-type they are holes.
[/important]

## 3. The p–n Junction Diode

Join a p-type and an n-type region and electrons diffuse from n to p,
leaving a thin **depletion region** with a built-in electric field
that opposes further flow.

| Bias | Connection | Behaviour |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forward | p-side to +, n-side to − | Depletion region narrows; current flows easily (after ~0.7 V for Si) |
| Reverse | p-side to −, n-side to + | Depletion region widins; only a tiny leakage current |

The asymmetric current–voltage curve is the diode's signature: it
conducts one way only — an electronic **one-way valve**.

```text
      Forward bias →  current flows
   +──▶|── -          (arrow = anode/p, bar = cathode/n)
      ◀─  Reverse: blocked
```

### Applications

- **Rectification:** converting AC to DC — a bridge of four diodes
  flips the negative half-cycle, giving the pulsating DC a phone
  charger then smooths.
- **Light emitting diode (LED):** forward current makes electrons fall
  into holes, emitting photons; the band gap sets the colour.
- **Solar cell:** light does the reverse — it knocks electrons loose,
  driving current the "wrong" way and delivering power.
- **Zener diode:** designed to conduct in reverse at a precise
  voltage, acting as a voltage reference.

## 4. The Transistor: Amplifier and Switch

A **bipolar junction transistor (BJT)** sandwiches three layers —
npn or pnp — giving emitter, base, and collector terminals. A small
base current controls a large collector current: the transistor
**amplifies**.

The more common **field-effect transistor (FET/MOSFET)** uses a
voltage on the gate to open or shut a channel between source and
drain. Because the gate draws almost no current, millions of them fit
in a microchip without melting it.

[tip]
A MOSFET gate is effectively a tiny capacitor. CMOS chips pair an
n-channel and a p-channel MOSFET so that one is always off — power is
drawn mainly while switching, which is why clock speed and voltage
dominate a chip's heat output.
[/tip]

Two working modes matter:

1. **Switch:** gate voltage on/off = 1/0 — the heart of digital
   logic. A modern processor executes billions of such switches per
   second.
2. **Amplifier:** a small signal at the input is reproduced as a
   large signal at the output — the heart of audio systems and radio
   transmitters.

## 5. Logic Gates: Computing With Switches

Combine transistors so that outputs depend on inputs by rule, and you
have **logic gates** — the alphabet of digital circuits.

| Gate | Output is 1 when | Boolean form |
| --- | --- | --- |
| AND | all inputs are 1 | A · B |
| OR | any input is 1 | A + B |
| NOT | input is 0 (inverts) | $\overline{A}$ |
| NAND | not all inputs are 1 | $\overline{A \cdot B}$ |
| NOR | no input is 1 | $\overline{A + B}$ |
| XOR | inputs differ | A ⊕ B |

@diagram flowchart id="half-adder" layout="hierarchical" aria="An XOR gate and an AND gate wired as a half adder"
node a label="A" shape="circle"
node b label="B" shape="circle"
node x label="XOR" shape="rect"
node n label="AND" shape="rect"
node s label="Sum" shape="round-rect"
node c label="Carry" shape="round-rect"

a -> x
b -> x
a -> n
b -> n
x -> s
n -> c
@/diagram

Wire XOR and AND in parallel on the same inputs (the diagram above)
and you get a **half adder** — the one-bit adding circuit that,
stacked, does arithmetic in every processor. NAND alone is
**functionally complete**: every other gate, and hence every computer
ever built, can be constructed from NAND gates alone.

## 6. From One Transistor to a Billion

- 1947 — the point-contact transistor (Bardeen, Brattain, Shockley;
  Nobel 1956) at Bell Labs.
- 1958 — the integrated circuit (Kilby at TI, Noyce at Fairchild)
  puts many transistors on one chip.
- 1965 — Moore's law: transistor counts double roughly every two
  years; the trend held for half a century.
- Today — smartphone chips hold tens of billions of transistors, each
  a MOSFET switch a few dozen atoms long.

[summary]
Semiconductors sit between conductors and insulators, and doping
turns pure silicon into n-type (electron carriers) or p-type (hole
carriers). A p–n junction conducts one way — the diode, basis of
rectifiers, LEDs, and solar cells. Transistors (BJT, MOSFET) amplify
or switch; as switches they build logic gates, whose rules (AND, OR,
NOT, NAND, NOR, XOR) compose into adders and processors. The entire
digital world is doped silicon, layered one switch at a time.
[/summary]
